ple who  want to be part of this because it's  history it's a highly significant event  and being part of it gives significance  to them they can say i was there they  can tell other people they were there  there's a little bit of stardust  sprinkled on them  but also and on top of that it confers  upon them a certain amount of of  immortality if you like they're part of  history  they were there when and whenever it  comes up on the television in the future  perhaps even after they're dead they are  part of history um so there are a very  large variety of motives and it's wrong  to assume that everyone who's there is a  royalist and it's wrong to assume that  everybody is there is there in respect  united in grief or whatever and i think  the danger is that we come to false  conclusions about the nature of our  society or even worse we begin to  enforce narrow notions of our society  because if you assume  that the uh the crowd is united uh in uh  monarchism in acceptance of the  institution and if you also believe that  the uh the crowd represents the nation  then you come to the conclusion that  anybody who is not united in grief isn't  really part of the nation is excluded  from the nation and so certain things  begin to be unsavable it's hard to  dissent it's hard to question uh  monacism it's hard to question issues of  colonialism and i think there is a  danger of creating a narrow view of our  society in which a plurality of voices  cannot be heard and certain things  cannot be spoken  i want to come back to that wider point  in a second but but i i saw in an  article that you'd written that you  reflected on the way that this moment  had um  if you like stirred up uh your own  memories of um  relatives passing i'm sure that there's  a  psychologist's uh definition uh word for  for this uh do you think that that is uh  something that's going on across the  country that people are sort of  they're this this is provoked if you  like uh a memory of sorrow for them  which is to do with their own lives well  absolutely i mean so for me yes when  when the queen died it made me think  about when my mother died and i remember  i my mum um lived in london i was coming  down on a train to see her i was told  she was very ill and she died before i  got there and so i felt for harry who  got there too late as well there was an  interesting poll which showed that 44 of  british people shed a tear about the  queen but the assumption was what that  meant was they were all royalists that  they were all shedding a tear because  they were invested in the queen now  things are much more complex than that  and therefore i think we need to be  careful of how we interpret uh the  genuine grief and the genuine sorry that  sorrow that many people had myself  included as indicating if you like a  certain  accepting certain views about society  buying into uh certain notions of  how we should be ruled and so on because  if we do that and i think as i say what  we do is  we begin not just  not to reflect society but to begin to  say that only certain views are  acceptable in society and i think that  would be both a misuse of the evidence  but also something which i don't think  is particularly good uh for a plural  society in an open society  just very briefly steve you've got about  30 seconds one of the things that struck  me is that actually  there's been a sort of evolution of some  what you might call popular rituals the  silence the the applause the flowers and  so on which aren't organized they're  just what people are doing is that  something new for us  no i mean well first of all i think the  important thing about these rituals is  they bring together the personal and the  political they are deeply felt there are  deep and meaningful emotions here but  always rituals commemorations of this  sort are ways of creating a national  identity a way of defining who we are  and what's acceptable in our society and  that's what we're seeing  it's not just history what we're seeing  is the making of history and that's why  it's so fascinating and so important  i would love to talk more about that but  i'm afraid we're out of time steve thank  you so much for that  news at 10 is next  but first let's look back at another  emotional day as the royal family joined  people across the country as they pay  their respects  [Music]  you
 

